92186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1045 "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e8bbfc2d-b5f4-57c0-9027-1b66b844109b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1061 20075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b7139a7-3f19-5d72-857b-7a01a291e46b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1061 20077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4921a5cc-f26d-5b7a-9865-e447eec1d53e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1061 20078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64cdc019-4015-5867-b014-691bcc52330a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1061 20081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e9a9912-8def-5a76-892f-0608e119d89c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1061 21410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b97f2fc-3025-5372-9dc4-19c05d084b76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1061 23514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab53090e-96b8-5dcc-b77f-6ff038689cac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n![](images/63/63e2c65a23a0680412a14084d543b6cc19c0529fd80863b09c99987d309eaa4f.svg) \nand the vector ![](images/b7/b7811b74d8d3075357925864b5be27f57b292dadca9c04d222954baa1e66ba2a.svg) Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector ![](images/13/13a28e543647ede7a88b30fb652bd7fba6c9adb8ee3142161a102de947ef2d2a.svg) is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1065 3557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1064 "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1065 3963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1067 "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284787 #edb/ref 1080 "14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "000e3561-1544-5dc9-8c11-17c48b422a41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284868 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284868 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284868 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "097c3642-1c4d-5f84-8e55-34a351e7fb55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284893 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284893 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284893 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cd337c9-0e8e-5c98-801f-ee9718e01ded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285075 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285075 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285075 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "201af8ed-db42-5e03-815b-dbf694b92e78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287077 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287077 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287077 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fad2ed4b-35b0-525e-8240-933b2bc19e1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186284787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186284868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186284893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186287077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186285075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287482 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a631f2f2-9eef-524a-8c00-16323ea7e0c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287815 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287815 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287815 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287815 #edb/ref 1071 "An eigenvector of $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186287815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac23d8ea-7894-5e1d-9e2b-923ff426fb50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287815 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $D$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Dv=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Dv & =\\lambda v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} & =6\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-8c+140 \\\\ -7c+154\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6c \\\\ 84\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8c+140 &=6c \\\\ -7c+154 &=84\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8c+140=6c\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7c+154=84\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186342321 #edb/ref 1080 "images/8b/8b688fcd0fd4e0eeee005687fcdc53fe96b671e551a99656df9cb77175f40df1.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342321 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342321 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09e12ff8-523f-5c8d-a706-ba8422caadff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343295 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ba/baab64be7493281733cf8b4b94736d4bb46a43e511b379e939abf0cd3d822510.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343295 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343295 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7af1fddd-ef2d-5429-8bef-94f40a541aa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343443 #edb/ref 1080 "images/75/75a8012b7823a0256e42a4db5fc40c3ea3a12226bd9989ab3de639c2ee271c98.pn